From: fish <lunarisfelix02@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 20:20:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] op7t-0223: IOSurface planes report their bytes per row (video)

Videos never played: mediaserverd died with SIGBUS in H264SW's copy into
its output frame (row step 2*dstBytesPerRow - width = -0x170, written as
unsigned 32-bit -> 4 GiB past the frame). Reproduced without video
(tools/cv420v_test.py): a 352x288 '420v' surface reports plane widths and
heights but bytes per row 0 for both planes.

IOSurface.framework (16.5 20F66) reads the lock result at IOSurfaceClient
+ 0x68; per plane i (record at result + 0x68 + i*0x80), disassembled from
the phone: width +0x00 (GetWidthOfPlane: client+0xd0), height +0x04
(+0xd4), offset +0x0c (GetBaseAddressOfPlane: +0xdc), bytes per row +0x10
(GetBytesPerRowOfPlane: +0xe0), bytes per element +0x18 (+0xe8), element
width/height +0x1a/+0x1b (+0xea/+0xeb); a byte at +0x54 (+0x124) equal to
1 makes both report 0. sweet_surface_fill_result wrote bytes per row only
at +0x08: now also at +0x10 (+0x08 kept).

Checked with tools/xnu-cc.py (CI flags).

Apply after 0222.
---
diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetSurface.cpp b/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetSurface.cpp
index c2879ac..59492fc 100644
--- a/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetSurface.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetSurface.cpp
@@ -721,6 +721,14 @@ sweet_surface_fill_result(SweetSurface *s, SweetSurfaceRef *ref, uint8_t *out)
 		put32(p, 0x00, pl->width);
 		put32(p, 0x04, pl->height);
 		put32(p, 0x08, pl->bytesPerRow);
+		/*
+		 * 0223: where IOSurface.framework (16.5 20F66) reads a plane's bytes per row: IOSurfaceGetBytesPerRowOfPlane ->
+		 * IOSurfaceClient + 0xe0 + i * 0x80 = this record + 0x10 (width +0x00, height +0x04, offset +0x0c, bytes per
+		 * element +0x18, element width/height +0x1a/+0x1b, as written here; a byte at +0x54 equal to 1 makes it
+		 * report 0). Only +0x08 had it: every plane said 0 bytes per row, and H264SW's copy into a '420v' frame
+		 * stepped 4 GiB past it (SIGBUS in mediaserverd, videos never played). +0x08 kept as it was.
+		 */
+		put32(p, 0x10, pl->bytesPerRow);
 		put32(p, 0x0c, pl->offset);
 		put32(p, 0x14, pl->size);
 		memcpy(p + 0x18, &pl->bytesPerElement, 2);
